---
/**
 * THE BRAND LOCKUP. Single source of truth for the wordmark + tagline.
 *
 * THE LOCKUP IS A VECTOR IMAGE, NOT LIVE TEXT (Antonio's decision "B",
 * 2026-08-03): live text is painted by each visitor's font engine, macOS
 * and Linux round glyph advances differently, and after three generations
 * of alignment work the last device pixel still wobbled between his screen
 * and the build's. Outlines rasterise identically everywhere.
 *
 * IT IS NOW AN <img>, NOT AN INLINE <svg> (H8, 2026-08-05).
 * The lockup is 15.6 KB of path data and it appeared on every page twice
 * (header + footer) and on every tunnel screen once: 455 copies, and 29% of
 * all the HTML this site ships. Every one of those bytes was uncacheable,
 * because it lived inside the HTML document, so a DJ browsing ten pages
 * downloaded the same logo ten times, and the offline precache paid for it
 * on all 53 precached routes.
 * As an external file it is fetched once, cached by the browser and by the
 * service worker, and shared by every page and every language.
 * WHAT MADE THIS SAFE: the lockup is a static, self-contained asset with its
 * own baked colours. It has no CSS dependency, inherits nothing, is never
 * recoloured per page and is never partially styled, so nothing was lost by
 * putting it behind an <img> boundary. If a future version of the lockup
 * needs to inherit currentColor or animate, this has to go back to inline.
 *
 * SOURCE OF TRUTH SINCE 2026-08-03 (his ruling: "I want it just like
 * this"): public/images/brand-lockup.svg is EXTRACTED FROM ANTONIO'S CANVA
 * MASTER, the design that also feeds his print/social art, not generated
 * from the web fonts any more (scripts/gen-lockup.py is retired, kept for
 * its history, and it wrote the file at its old path under src/components).
 * The extraction (recorded here because the Canva file will change again):
 *   - his export ships the shield as an embedded BITMAP; we take only the
 *     three vector text groups and keep the site's own vector shield
 *     (public/images/logo-shield.svg) for every shield slot.
 *   - his colours (#ebebeb / #f74921 / #a7a89a) are remapped to the site
 *     tokens (#f3f1ec / #ff4d2e / #9a978f) so GIG matches the house red
 *     used everywhere else. Geometry his, palette ours.
 *   - his tagline ink was 1 unit narrower than the wordmark; it is
 *     stretched 0.19% so first/last ink edges match exactly, which is the
 *     alignment rule he checks by eye.
 *   - the viewBox is trimmed to the ink bbox, so CSS widths size the ink.
 * TO UPDATE: get his new Canva SVG export (zip it, .svg uploads are
 * rejected by the chat), re-run the extraction, commit to
 * public/images/brand-lockup.svg, and bump the sw.js CACHE version so
 * installed devices stop serving the old one. Do NOT hand-edit.
 *
 * AND THAT INSTRUCTION WAS INCOMPLETE UNTIL 2026-08-06. A CACHE bump has
 * authority over the service worker's store and none at all over the BROWSER's
 * HTTP cache, and this file lives under /images/, which public/_headers gave 30
 * days. So a corrected logo reached installed devices on the next visit and
 * ordinary returning visitors up to a month later, while the line above implied
 * the bump covered it. public/_headers now carries a one-day rule for this exact
 * path, ahead of /images/*, and the reasoning is recorded there. The bump is
 * still required; it is just no longer the only thing standing between a fixed
 * logo and the people looking at it.
 *
 * The widths below carry over from the approved sizes; the new lockup's
 * aspect (0.2044) is within 1% of the old (0.2059), so nothing moved.
 *
 * TWO VARIANTS, TWO WIDTH REFERENCES, still deliberate:
 *   full    the site header AND the rescue tunnel bar
 *   footer  sized to the SOCIAL ROW (his rectangle rule: wordmark, tagline
 *           and social row are ONE width; change the social buttons and the
 *           footer sizes here must be re-derived)
 *
 * ACCESSIBILITY. alt is EMPTY, on purpose, in both variants. Every place
 * this component is used it sits inside a link that already carries
 * aria-label={S.aria.brandHome} (Base.astro's .nav-brand and .foot-lockup,
 * Tunnel.astro's .brand), next to a shield <img> that is also alt="". Real
 * alt text here would make those links announce the brand twice. The old
 * inline SVG's own role="img" + aria-label is still in the file and is now
 * correctly ignored, because an <img> does not expose its content's ARIA.
 * If the lockup is ever used OUTSIDE a labelled link, that use needs real
 * alt and this component needs an `alt` prop, not a hardcoded string.
 */

interface Props {
  variant?: 'full' | 'footer';
}
const { variant = 'full' } = Astro.props;
const cls = variant === 'footer' ? 'smg-foot' : 'smg-full';

// EXPLICIT DIMENSIONS = NO LAYOUT SHIFT. An inline <svg> carries its aspect
// in the document already; an <img> does not until it loads, and the lockup
// is the widest thing in the nav, so an unsized box would jolt the whole bar
// on first paint. These are the rendered pixel sizes at each variant's
// DEFAULT breakpoint: the stylesheet width below x the lockup's own aspect
// (129.74 / 634.85 = 0.204363). The narrower breakpoints only shrink the
// box; the RATIO is what prevents the shift, and .smg-brand pins that ratio
// in CSS as well, so the box is exact at every width, loaded or not.
const DIMS = {
  full: { w: 150, h: 31 },    // 9.4009rem  = 150.41 x 30.74 px
  footer: { w: 173, h: 35 },  // 10.8161rem = 173.06 x 35.37 px
};
const d = variant === 'footer' ? DIMS.footer : DIMS.full;
---
<img
  class:list={['smg-brand', cls]}
  src="/images/brand-lockup.svg"
  width={d.w}
  height={d.h}
  alt=""
  decoding="async"
/>

<style is:global>
  /* The width rules are UNCHANGED from the inline-SVG version, deliberately:
     H8 changed how the lockup is DELIVERED and nothing about how it looks.
     `aspect-ratio` is the one addition. It is the asset's viewBox restated in
     CSS, so the box is the exact height the inline <svg> computed, even before
     the file has arrived. `.smg-brand svg { width: 100% }` is gone with the
     inline SVG it existed for. */
  .smg-brand {
    display: block;
    height: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 634.85 / 129.74;
  }

  /* width = 6.7390 x the wordmark font-size each variant used as live text,
     so nothing moved visually in the conversion. */
  .smg-full { width: 9.4009rem; }            /* 1.395rem word */
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .smg-full { width: 11.0250rem; } }  /* 1.636rem */
  @media (max-width: 400px) { .smg-full { width: 8.9763rem; } }   /* 1.332rem */

  .smg-foot { width: 10.8161rem; }           /* 1.605rem word */
  @media (max-width: 414px) { .smg-foot { width: 8.9696rem; } }   /* 1.331rem */
</style>
